  {
   "name": "a control's name and its value are two slots: a filled field shows what it holds plus name:\"…\", an empty one falls back to the name, and a component-library select (a button that displays the chosen value) is the same case",
   "$comment": "Measured on a real framework-built address form. Its inputs set no id, no name, no placeholder and no aria-label — the field name is a sibling <label> with no `for` inside the field's own wrapper — so five textField lines carried nothing to tell them apart and the only way to pick one was its position in the DOM. Doing that put a city into the street box while `act type` reported textLanded=exact focusLanded=self. The accname walk now reaches that label; this case pins that its result never DISPLACES the value, which was the first cut's regression: five filled fields projected as their own names with nothing saying what any of them contained. The `select` node is the shape that forced this beyond text fields: five such dropdowns projected as `button \"<value>\"` … — five values with nothing saying which field each belonged to.",
   "expect": {
    "compact": [
     "@root container [0,0 1080x2400]",
     "@city textField \"Warszawa\" [60,400 960x120] tappable name:\"Miasto\"",
     "@street textField \"Miasto\" [60,600 960x120] tappable",
     "@code textField \"12-345\" [60,800 960x120] tappable placeholder:\"Postcode\"",
     "@education button \"Bachelor's\" [60,1000 960x120] tappable collapsed name:\"Education level\""
    ]
   },
